    purpose: "Recover the same pending or already-consumed message batch without losing or logically duplicating delivery",
    settlement: "A stable run-and-boundary command receipt returns the identical batch after a crash between registry CAS and invocation recording; acknowledged messages do not reappear at later boundaries",
    steps: "restart restores native shared thread state -> Cognate replays recorded run steps -> consume command revalidates current recipient Team and Assignment -> matching receipt returns same ordered batch or new boundary commits next eligible batch -> agent continues at a safe model decision",
    variants: "stale Team membership, cancelled Assignment and private scope mismatch deny incorporation; retry cannot widen captured tools or mode; provider call is never interrupted"
}

Instance j_expert_parallel of "CanonicalJourney" {
    actor: "User or OperatorAgent",
    capabilities: "execution.runtime.inspect, expert.converse, team.converse, subagent.start, team.retry, team.handoff",
    evidence_class: "declared",
    initiating_condition: "The operator wants independent coding or Expert work to overlap without allowing shared or uncertain workspace mutations to race",
    journey_id: "J-EXPERT-PARALLEL",
    precondition: "The native runtime exposes a configured maxConcurrentRuns value (default 1; opt-in values 2, 4, or 8); each admitted mutating run resolves to a canonical execution-world and workspace-root identity; unresolved identity blocks admission without effects until safely resolved; multi-process operation is exposed only behind a verified ownership configuration backed by a published fenced run-write ownership release",
    purpose: "Bound process-level run admission while permitting overlap only for work with disjoint canonical workspace roots; under the verified multi-process configuration, one store serves several Workbench processes with authoritative per-run ownership",
    settlement: "execution.runtime.inspect reports the effective configured capacity and admission policy without claiming active-worker counts; two Experts on distinct workspaces demonstrably overlap when capacity permits, while same, aliased, or nested roots serialize; unresolved root identity blocks admission without effects; restart, mutation ownership, Team invariants, authorization, and each run's captured model route remain intact; under the verified multi-process configuration a killed process's in-flight effect is never blindly repeated by the recovering process — the recovery records the ambiguity and completes only from explicit reconciliation, and a stale owner's late run-scoped write is fenced at the store with nothing appended",
    steps: "operator inspects native execution capacity -> direct coding, Expert, Team, delegated, resumed, and recovered run entries pass through the same nativeExecutionSlots admission boundary -> runtime resolves execution-world and canonical root identities -> unresolved identity blocks admission before effects -> only runs with non-overlapping known roots may occupy distinct slots -> operator inspects run evidence and the configured capacity -> under multi-process operation a crashed owner's run is recovered by the surviving process, whose ambiguous prior-generation effect requires reconciliation and whose store fences the killed owner's late writes",
    variants: "default capacity one is serial; configured capacity accepts only 1, 2, 4, or 8; same-root, alias-root, and ancestor/descendant-root cases serialize; unresolved root identity blocks admission without effects until safely resolved; read-only work does not bypass human approval for protected effects; existing ParallelPlan recommendations remain advisory; Team-level maximum concurrency and batch delegation policy are not established by this journey; single-process deployments keep the historical restart semantics — multi-process semantics apply only when the verified ownership configuration is enabled"
}

Instance j_expert_model_parallel of "CanonicalJourney" {
    actor: "User or OperatorAgent",
    capabilities: "execution.runtime.inspect, expert.converse, team.converse, team.handoff, team.retry",
    evidence_class: "observed",
    initiating_condition: "The operator enables bounded native capacity for independent Expert model decisions while retaining conservative exclusion of workspace effects",
    journey_id: "J-EXPERT-MODEL-PARALLEL",
    precondition: "A supported native runtime accepts authoritative asynchronous admission; capacity defaults to one with opt-in two, four or eight; a genuine system-started Expert run resolves its registered unfinished Assignment and matching attempt before eligibility is granted",
    purpose: "Overlap only Expert decisions whose frozen tools contain the intrinsic expert_return and no workspace, delegation, messaging or memory tools",
    settlement: "Two independently routed model-only Expert runs overlap at capacity two and return attributable durable results; forged tool calls cannot widen the frozen allowlist; all tool-bearing and ordinary coding runs share one exclusion key; no active-worker count or general workspace parallelism is claimed",
    steps: "operator configures host capacity and inspects effective policy -> native admission resolves the registered Assignment after application readiness -> only intrinsic-return assignments receive independent slots -> all other work retains conservative shared exclusion -> native runs return correlated handoffs -> operator inspects evidence",
    variants: "unknown or stale Assignment, attempt, sender or Team membership blocks admission before model effects; event-only agent.config bypasses application-readiness lookup to preserve seed boot; cancellation and recovery use the same native admission path; current frozen tools and model route survive profile edits; capacity remains a startup setting; Team-specific capacities and disjoint workspace effect parallelism remain deferred under J-EXPERT-PARALLEL"
}

Instance j_auth_session of "CanonicalJourney" {
    actor: "Operator",
    capabilities: "identity.session.issue, identity.session.revoke, identity.inspect",
    evidence_class: "proposed",
    initiating_condition: "The operator replaces client-claimed identities with server-issued durable sessions",
    journey_id: "J-AUTH-SESSION",
    precondition: "A durable identity ledger exists; the browser entry and every remote request must present a session the server itself issued",
    purpose: "Make the effective caller a durable, revocable server-side fact instead of a client-supplied string",
    settlement: "Every admitted request resolves to a live unexpired unrevoked session bound to one identity; a revoked or expired session is refused with no durable effect; a captured token replayed after revocation produces no durable write anywhere in the store",
    steps: "boot seeds the operator identity and issues one session for this browser entry -> every request authenticates against the durable session ledger -> operator may issue further sessions for provisioned identities -> operator revokes a session -> the revoked session's replayed request is refused before any capability or service mutation -> boot reconciles expired sessions",
    variants: "the raw development bearer format is refused in the production posture unless an explicit dev opt-in is armed; revocation takes effect without restart; session tokens exist in cleartext only in the issued entry and the holder's memory, the ledger stores verified hashes; an unknown token, an altered token or a session for a missing identity is refused"
}

Instance j_auth_tenant_isolation of "CanonicalJourney" {
    actor: "Operator",
    capabilities: "identity.session.issue",
    evidence_class: "proposed",
    initiating_condition: "The operator provisions an identity in a second tenant on one shared store",
    journey_id: "J-AUTH-TENANT-ISOLATION",
    precondition: "Sessions bind each caller to the tenant of its durable identity; public projections and run, artifact and stream reads are tenant-partitioned by the framework",
    purpose: "Prove one tenant's session cannot observe or mutate another tenant's work on a shared store",
    settlement: "Each authenticated caller sees only its own tenant's partition: its projections, runs, artifacts and streams; a cross-tenant replay or a forged tenant claim is refused with no durable effect; an empty foreign partition is indistinguishable from an absent one",
    steps: "operator issues a session for a second-tenant identity -> both sessions drive the same shared store -> each caller's projection reads stay inside its tenant prefix -> cross-tenant run, artifact and event reads are refused as not found -> a client-claimed tenant string without a session is refused -> operator inspects both partitions",
    variants: "a session token from one tenant cannot be replayed into another tenant's context; the framework's tenant partitioning is observed end to end, not assumed; multi-tenant application seeding (per-tenant Experts and schedules) is out of scope and not claimed"
}

Instance j_rw_aggregate_containment of "CanonicalJourney" {
    actor: "Operator",
    capabilities: "settings.changeContainment, workspace.contained.select, process.exec",
    evidence_class: "proposed",
    initiating_condition: "The operator configures aggregate resource ceilings for contained executions",
    journey_id: "J-RW-AGGREGATE-CONTAINMENT",
    precondition: "A contained workspace is selected and its provider owns the OS boundary; the aggregate containment ledger is the durable admission truth across processes",
    purpose: "Bound aggregate contained resource consumption across processes so exhaustion of a configured quota refuses new work instead of silently widening it",
    settlement: "New contained work that would exceed the aggregate concurrent-execution ceiling or the aggregate CPU-second window budget is refused before any child process is launched, from any process sharing the store; each admitted execution runs under its kernel CPU and address-space caps; a cancelled or completed execution releases its aggregate slot; a crashed holder's stale slot is reaped after its lease and accounted conservatively",
    steps: "operator configures aggregate containment quotas -> every contained execution acquires a durable aggregate slot under the quota before launch -> admitted executions run under kernel per-execution CPU and address-space limits -> completion, cancellation and crash release or reap the slot durably -> exhausted quota fails closed for new work across processes -> operator inspects the aggregate ledger",
    variants: "an unconfigured quota keeps historical uncontained-aggregate behavior; a configured quota with kernel limit tooling unavailable inside the world refuses the execution rather than running uncapped; a CPU-capped execution is recorded as capped and booked conservatively; per-process memory accounting is bounded by the kernel address-space cap and the aggregate concurrency ceiling, not measured; local uncontained workspaces are not aggregate-contained and never were"
}
